Q: Ternary Operator with Exceptions? A: <p>Admittedly, this might be a bit of overengineering, but we could define a wrapper <code>attempt()</code> that handles both cases (item access and attribute access) by providing a <code>fallback</code> value in case of exceptions, and thus simulates a ternary operator:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def attempt(*, fallback, get_from):

    class Wrapper:

        @staticmethod
        def __getitem__(i):
            try:
                result = get_from[i]
            except IndexError:
                result = fallback
            return result

        @staticmethod
        def __getattribute__(name):
            try:
                result = getattr(get_from, name)
            except AttributeError:
                result = fallback
            return result

    return Wrapper()
</code></pre>
<p>This could be applied as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    # Indexing example
    sequence = [42, 3, 2, 1, 0]
    val = attempt(fallback=sequence[0], get_from=sequence)[1]
    print(val)  # Succeeds: prints 3
    val = attempt(fallback=sequence[0], get_from=sequence)[10]
    print(val)  # Fails: prints 42 (fallback value)

    # Attribute access example
    from types import SimpleNamespace
    names = SimpleNamespace(family=&quot;Doe&quot;, default=&quot;John&quot;)
    val = attempt(fallback=names.default, get_from=names).family
    print(val)  # Succeeds: prints &quot;Doe&quot;
    val = attempt(fallback=names.default, get_from=names).first
    print(val)  # Fails: prints &quot;John&quot; (fallback value)
</code></pre>
<p>Of course, we could likewise define <code>fallback</code> to provide the default <em>index</em> or <em>attribute</em> rather than the default <em>value</em>:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def attempt(*, fallback, get_from):

    class Wrapper:

        @staticmethod
        def __getitem__(i):
            try:
                result = get_from[i]
            except IndexError:
                result = get_from[fallback]
            return result

        @staticmethod
        def __getattribute__(name):
            try:
                result = getattr(get_from, name)
            except AttributeError:
                result = getattr(get_from, fallback)
            return result

    return Wrapper()
</code></pre>
<p>Which could then be applied as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    # Indexing example
    sequence = [42, 3, 2, 1, 0]
    val = attempt(fallback=0, get_from=sequence)[1]
    print(val)  # Succeeds: prints 3
    val = attempt(fallback=0, get_from=sequence)[10]
    print(val)  # Fails: prints 42 (value at fallback index)

    # Attribute access example
    from types import SimpleNamespace
    names = SimpleNamespace(family=&quot;Doe&quot;, default=&quot;John&quot;)
    val = attempt(fallback=&quot;default&quot;, get_from=names).family
    print(val)  # Succeeds: prints &quot;Doe&quot;
    val = attempt(fallback=&quot;default&quot;, get_from=names).first
    print(val)  # Fails: prints &quot;John&quot; (value at fallback attribute)
</code></pre>
